<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A namespaced attribute is published under its LOCAL name.

  The store reduces every edge target to its last `:`/`.` segment before it looks
  anything up (`parallel-indexer.ts:3375-3390`, `extractLeaf`), so an edge spelled
  `xml:space` is searched for as `space`. While the DECLARATION was published as
  `xml:space`, nothing in the project matched that search: the attribute's own
  `contains` edge stayed unbound, or bound to a declaration of `space` in some
  unrelated file, because `resolveContainsTargetStmt`
  (`parallel-indexer.ts:2354-2360`) only ORDERS same-file first and never FILTERS
  to the edge's file.

  Measured through the real indexer on the 120-file draw `08d7fb24a87da7e7`
  (`validation/m14/fixtures/xml/store-binding-truth.mts`): of 3,803 `contains`
  rows, 179 were unbound and 35 bound to another file; 178 of the 179 had a
  project-unique declaration of the LOCAL name, so the declaration was present the
  whole time and the spelling hid it. The top unbound targets were `xml:space`,
  `rdf:resource`, `xlink:href`, `rdf:about`, `cc:Work`, `dc:format` — every one a
  prefixed name.

  `id` and `fill` are the CONTROL: unprefixed, already local, and must be
  unchanged by any fix. An implementation that stripped the last segment instead
  of the first (`inkscape:label` -> `label` but also `a:b:c` -> `c`, or worse
  `foo.bar` -> `bar` on an unprefixed name containing a dot) would pass every
  prefixed row here and fail these two.

  `xlink` is here because `xmlns:` declarations are published by the SAME call
  site and were ALREADY local (`name = namespacePrefix`), so they are the control
  that says the convention is one rule and not two.
-->
<svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" viewBox="0 0 10 10">
  <g xml:space="preserve" id="g1">
    <path d="M0 0" fill="red"/>
  </g>
  <use xlink:href="#g1" x="1"/>
</svg>